"""Grade 4: the Creed, lesson by lesson, written as a catechist would plan it."""
R = {
'4-1-1': dict(
 idea='The Creed is the Church’s short summary of the faith, handed down from the apostles. “Creed” comes from the Latin credo, “I believe.” We pray the Nicene Creed at Sunday Mass and the Apostles’ Creed at Baptism and in the Rosary.',
 hook='Ask a child to explain their favorite sport in one sentence. Then ask, “How would you sum up the whole Catholic faith in one minute?” The early Christians faced the same problem and the Creed was their answer.',
 activity='“Creed in order” (25 min). Write the twelve articles of the Apostles’ Creed on strips. In teams, put them in order and sort them into three groups: Father, Son, Holy Spirit. Then read the Creed aloud together and mark each place where it is about God and where it is about us.',
 teach=['The Creed is a summary of the faith (CCC 187). The Apostles’ Creed is the old baptismal creed of Rome; the Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed comes from the councils of 325 and 381 (CCC 193–195).',
        'It has three parts, for the three Persons of the Trinity: Father and creation, Son and redemption, Holy Spirit and the Church and eternal life (CCC 190).',
        'Romans 10:9–10 links believing in the heart with confessing with the lips. “I believe” is personal, while at Mass we say “We believe” because faith is shared (CCC 167).',
        'Do not reduce the Creed to memorizing. Ask at each article, “What does this change about how I live?”'],
 misc=['“The Creed is just a prayer to recite.”', '“Each person makes up their own creed.”'],
 mem='“I believe in God, the Father almighty, creator of heaven and earth.” (Apostles’ Creed, first article)',
 assess='Ask children to say the first three articles from memory and to name the three Persons the Creed is organized around.'),
'4-1-2': dict(
 idea='Faith is both God’s gift and our free answer. It means trusting God and believing everything he has revealed because he cannot deceive or be deceived. Abraham and Mary show what faith looks like.',
 hook='Do a trust fall in pairs, or ask: “Would you step onto a bridge if someone you trusted said it was safe?” Faith in God is like that, but God is infinitely more trustworthy.',
 activity='“Heroes of faith” (25 min). In groups, read Hebrews 11:1–3 and then one story: Abraham (Genesis 12:1–4), Noah (Genesis 6–7), Mary (Luke 1:38). Each group makes a one-minute newscast: “What did they trust God to do? What did it cost?”',
 teach=['Faith is a gift of God and a human act (CCC 153–155). We choose to believe, but we could not without grace.',
        'Faith is certain because God is truth (CCC 156–157). It is reasonable, and it seeks understanding (CCC 158–159). Faith and science do not contradict.',
        'Abraham is “the father of all believers” (CCC 144–146) and Mary is the model of faith (CCC 148–149). Hebrews 11 lists many more.',
        'Faith must be lived and grow. We nourish it with Scripture, the sacraments and prayer, and it can be lost if neglected (CCC 162).'],
 misc=['“Faith means believing without any reason.”', '“If I have doubts, my faith is gone.”'],
 mem='“Now faith is the assurance of things hoped for, the conviction of things not seen.” (Hebrews 11:1)',
 assess='Ask: “Is faith a gift or a choice? Give one example from Abraham or Mary.”'),
'4-1-3': dict(
 idea='There is one God in three Persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. The three are distinct, equal and not three gods. The Trinity is the central mystery of our faith and shows that God is love.',
 hook='Trace the Sign of the Cross slowly. Ask, “Who do we name?” Then ask, “Is that three gods?” Take answers, then say: today we find out why the answer is no.',
 activity='“Good and bad pictures” (25 min). Show common analogies (the shamrock, water as ice/liquid/steam, the sun with light and heat). For each, children decide: What does it help show? Where does it fail (modalism, partialism)? Then draw the Trinity’s true image: a circle of love, giving and receiving. Read Matthew 28:19 and 2 Corinthians 13:13.',
 teach=['The Trinity is “the central mystery of Christian faith and life” (CCC 234). We know it only because God revealed it in Jesus (CCC 237).',
        'God is one in being (substance) and three in Persons (CCC 253–254). The Father is not the Son, the Son is not the Spirit, yet each is fully God.',
        'John says “God is love” (1 John 4:8). Love needs someone to love, and in the Trinity God is a communion of love from all eternity (CCC 221).',
        'Every analogy limps. St Patrick’s shamrock helps with “three in one” but can suggest God has parts. Say it is a mystery we can know but never exhaust.'],
 misc=['“God is one Person who plays three roles.”', '“The Father is greater than the Son and Spirit.”'],
 mem='“In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.” (Sign of the Cross; Matthew 28:19)',
 assess='Ask children to explain, in their own words, why the Trinity is not three gods, and to name what each Person is traditionally said to do (create, redeem, sanctify).'),
'4-2-1': dict(
 idea='God created everything out of nothing, freely and out of love. Creation is good, ordered and meant to praise him. Faith and science answer different questions: science how, faith who and why.',
 hook='Hold up a painting and ask, “Who made this? Can I see the artist?” Then look at a leaf or a photo of space. “What can this tell us about its maker?”',
 activity='“Seven days, one message” (30 min). Make a seven-column poster for Genesis 1. Under each day, children write what God made and draw it. Under it add the repeated refrain “And God saw that it was good.” Discuss: what is the author teaching? (God is the source, creation is good, humans are special). Compare it with Psalm 104.',
 teach=['God created the world freely, “out of nothing,” by his word (CCC 296–298). Creation is not an accident or a part of God.',
        'Genesis 1 teaches who made the world and why, not a science textbook (CCC 337–349). The Church sees no conflict between faith and genuine science (CCC 159, 283–284).',
        'Everything is “very good” (Genesis 1:31) and shows God’s wisdom (CCC 299, 339). Creation is a gift and we are stewards of it (CCC 2415–2418).',
        'Respond to questions about evolution simply: the Church allows that God may have used natural processes, but teaches that each human soul is directly created by God (CCC 366).'],
 misc=['“Catholics must reject science.”', '“Creation was made from God’s own substance.”'],
 mem='“In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth.” (Genesis 1:1)',
 assess='Ask: “What does ‘out of nothing’ mean? Name two things Genesis 1 teaches about creation.”'),
'4-2-2': dict(
 idea='God made angels, pure spirits who serve and praise him, and humans, who are body and soul in the image of God. Each person has a guardian angel and a soul that never dies.',
 hook='Ask, “What is invisible but real?” (air, love, a song). Say that angels are real beings we cannot see, and that you are a mix: a body you can see and a soul you cannot.',
 activity='“Angels and us” Venn (25 min). A Venn diagram labelled Angels, Humans and Both. Children place cards: has a body, has a soul, has intellect, has will, serves God, was made in his image, can die, never dies. Discuss. Then pray the Guardian Angel prayer.',
 teach=['Angels are spiritual, personal, immortal creatures with intellect and will (CCC 328–330). Scripture shows them as messengers (Luke 1:26–38) and protectors (Psalm 91:11; Matthew 18:10).',
        'The three archangels named in Scripture are Michael, Gabriel and Raphael (feast September 29). Each person has a guardian angel (CCC 336).',
        'Humans are body and soul united as one person (CCC 362–365) and made in God’s image (CCC 355–357). The soul is immortal and directly created by God (CCC 366).',
        'Some angels freely rebelled and became demons (CCC 391–395). Teach this briefly and without fear: Jesus has already won.'],
 misc=['“People become angels when they die.”', '“Angels are cute babies with wings.”'],
 mem='Angel of God, my guardian dear, to whom God’s love commits me here, ever this day be at my side, to light and guard, to rule and guide. Amen.',
 assess='Ask children to name two ways angels and humans are alike and two ways they differ.'),
'4-2-3': dict(
 idea='Adam and Eve freely disobeyed God, and sin and death entered the world. Their sin wounded human nature, and we are born needing redemption. But God immediately promised a Savior.',
 hook='Show a nice, clean white cloth and spill a little water or ink on it. “What happens when something spoils?” Say sin spoiled God’s good creation, and ask who could fix it.',
 activity='“The fall in four scenes” (25 min). Read Genesis 3 in four parts: the temptation, the sin, the hiding, the promise (3:15). For each, children draw and name what changed (trust, shame, blame, hope). Underline the first word of hope in Genesis 3:15 (“enmity… he will strike your head”). Trace it to Jesus and Mary.',
 teach=['Original sin: Adam and Eve freely disobeyed and lost original holiness (CCC 397–400). We inherit a fallen human nature, not their personal guilt (CCC 404–405).',
        'The effects: ignorance, suffering, death and an inclination to sin (concupiscence) (CCC 405–409). Baptism removes original sin but the inclination remains.',
        'Genesis 3:15 is the “protoevangelium,” the first announcement of the Gospel: a descendant of the woman will defeat the serpent (CCC 410–411). Christ is the new Adam, Mary the new Eve (CCC 411).',
        'Be honest and calm: the story explains why the world has evil without blaming God. It ends in hope.'],
 misc=['“Eve’s sin was eating an apple.”', '“Babies are born guilty of Adam’s personal sin.”'],
 mem='“I will put enmity between you and the woman… he shall bruise your head.” (Genesis 3:15)',
 assess='Ask: “What did the first sin break? What did God promise right away?”'),
'4-3-1': dict(
 idea='“Conceived by the Holy Spirit, born of the Virgin Mary.” The Son of God took a human nature in Mary’s womb. This is the Incarnation, the greatest event in history.',
 hook='Read Luke 1:26–38 dramatically with three readers. Stop at “How can this be?” Ask, “What would you have said?” then finish with Mary’s answer.',
 activity='“Annunciation in action” (25 min). Act the scene, then freeze-frame it. Each child steps in as Mary and says what she is thinking. Then compare Matthew’s account (Joseph, Matthew 1:18–25) with Luke’s (Mary). Fill a two-column chart: “What Matthew tells,” “What Luke tells.”',
 teach=['The Incarnation: the Son of God took flesh (CCC 461–463) to save us, show us God’s love, be our model and make us partakers of the divine nature (CCC 457–460).',
        'The virginal conception shows that Jesus is God’s Son and the new creation is God’s work (CCC 496–498). Mary remained a virgin before, during and after the birth (CCC 499–507).',
        'Mary’s “yes” (Luke 1:38) is the model of faith and freedom (CCC 494). Jesus is one Person with two natures, divine and human (CCC 464–469).',
        'Distinguish the Immaculate Conception (Mary’s conception) from the virginal conception (Jesus’). This confuses many adults too.'],
 misc=['“Immaculate Conception means Jesus’ conception.”', '“Jesus became God at his baptism.”'],
 mem='“Behold, I am the handmaid of the Lord; let it be done to me according to your word.” (Luke 1:38)',
 assess='Ask: “Who is the Father of Jesus’ human life? Who is his mother? Why does this matter?”'),
'4-3-2': dict(
 idea='“Suffered under Pontius Pilate, was crucified, died and was buried; on the third day he rose.” Jesus freely gave his life to forgive our sins, and the Resurrection shows that death and sin are defeated.',
 hook='Read part of John 19:16–30 with the lights low. Pause on “It is finished.” Ask, “Was that defeat or victory?” Then go quiet.',
 activity='“Holy Week map” (30 min). Make a timeline Palm Sunday to Easter Sunday. For each day add one event and one sign we see in church today. Draw the three “days” of the Triduum. Use Luke 24 to tell the Easter morning story. Plan a Holy Week prayer calendar.',
 teach=['Jesus died “for our sins” according to the Scriptures (1 Corinthians 15:3; CCC 599–605). He was not a victim of fate but gave his life freely (CCC 609–614). The responsibility belongs to all sinners, not to any one people (CCC 597–598).',
        'The Resurrection is both historical and transcendent: the tomb was empty and he appeared to many, in a glorified body (CCC 639–646). It is the foundation of our faith (CCC 651, 1 Corinthians 15:14).',
        'He “descended to the dead” to free the righteous who waited (CCC 632–635). The Cross is also a sacrifice, renewed in the Mass (CCC 1366).',
        'Take care with the Passion narratives, which have been misused to blame the Jews. The Church firmly rejects this (CCC 597).'],
 misc=['“The Jews killed Jesus.”', '“The Resurrection is only a symbol.”'],
 mem='“Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures… he was raised on the third day.” (1 Corinthians 15:3–4)',
 assess='Ask children to tell the events from Holy Thursday to Easter in order and say what each reveals about God’s love.'),
'4-3-3': dict(
 idea='“He ascended into heaven and is seated at the right hand of the Father; he will come again to judge the living and the dead.” Jesus reigns now, is present in his Church, and will return in glory.',
 hook='Ask: “Where is Jesus right now?” Take answers (heaven, with us, in the Eucharist). Then read Acts 1:9–11 and ask why the disciples stared at the sky.',
 activity='“Already and not yet” (25 min). Two columns: “Jesus has already…” (risen, ascended, sent the Spirit, given the Eucharist) and “Jesus will yet…” (return, judge, make all things new). Children fill with Scripture clues (Matthew 28:20; Acts 1:11; Matthew 25:31–46). Then write one thing to do while we wait.',
 teach=['At the Ascension Jesus enters into glory and reigns at the Father’s right hand (CCC 659–664). He did not leave us: “I am with you always” (Matthew 28:20).',
        'He will come again to judge the living and the dead (CCC 678–679). The judgment concerns love: “I was hungry and you gave me food” (Matthew 25:31–46).',
        'We do not know the day or hour (CCC 673–674). Our task is to be ready, to love and to spread the Gospel.',
        'Avoid frightening children with end-times speculation. Stress hope: Jesus returns as King and Friend.'],
 misc=['“Jesus left and is no longer with us.”', '“We can predict the date of the end.”'],
 mem='“Behold, I am with you always, to the close of the age.” (Matthew 28:20)',
 assess='Ask children to explain where Jesus is now, how he is present, and what he will do when he returns.'),
'4-4-1': dict(
 idea='The Holy Spirit is the third Person of the Trinity, “the Lord, the giver of life.” He inspired the Scriptures, formed the Church and lives in us. Fire, wind, water, oil and the dove are signs of his work.',
 hook='Fan a candle flame safely or blow a feather. “You cannot see wind but you can see what it does.” Ask where the Spirit has been at work in the stories they know.',
 activity='“Symbols of the Spirit” trail (30 min). Stations: water (fill a bowl), oil (smell chrism-scented oil), fire (candle), wind (feathers), dove (picture of the baptism of Jesus), seal (stamp). At each, read a verse (John 3:5, 1 John 2:20, Acts 2:3, John 3:8, Mark 1:10, Ephesians 1:13) and write what the sign says about the Spirit.',
 teach=['The Holy Spirit is God, equal to the Father and Son (CCC 243–248, 685). We pray, “I believe in the Holy Spirit, the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father and the Son.”',
        'He spoke through the prophets and inspired Scripture (CCC 687–688, 702–716). He was active at the Annunciation, the baptism of Jesus and Pentecost.',
        'He is the “soul of the Church” and gives gifts and fruits (CCC 797–798, 1830–1832). Learn the seven gifts: wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, fear of the Lord.',
        'The Spirit is a Person, not an energy. Use personal language: he teaches, comforts, guides.'],
 misc=['“The Holy Spirit is a force.”', '“The Spirit only came at Pentecost.”'],
 mem='Come, Holy Spirit, fill the hearts of your faithful, and kindle in them the fire of your love.',
 assess='Ask children to name three symbols of the Holy Spirit and say what each shows.'),
'4-4-2': dict(
 idea='The Church is the Body of Christ. Jesus is the head and we are members, each with different gifts. Everyone is needed, and what happens to one member affects all.',
 hook='Ask a volunteer to try to write while their hand is tied behind their back, or hop on one foot. “What happens when a part is missing?” Read 1 Corinthians 12:12.',
 activity='“Parish body” (25 min). Draw a large body. Children label parts with real parish roles: eyes (lectors), hands (volunteers, servers), voices (choir), feet (missionaries, visiting the sick), heart (those who pray), and add their own. Write one gift each child has to offer.',
 teach=['Paul compares the Church to a body (1 Corinthians 12:12–27; CCC 787–791). Christ is the head, the Spirit the soul, and we are members joined by Baptism.',
        'The Church is also the People of God and the Temple of the Spirit (CCC 781–786, 797). These images complement each other.',
        '“If one member suffers, all suffer together” (1 Corinthians 12:26). The Body of Christ is nourished by the Eucharist (CCC 805–807).',
        'Help children see that no gift is small: the smile of a greeter matters as much as a homily.'],
 misc=['“The Church is just the building.”', '“I can follow Jesus without the Church.”'],
 mem='“Now you are the body of Christ and individually members of it.” (1 Corinthians 12:27)',
 assess='Ask each child to name a gift they have and how it serves the Body.'),
'4-4-3': dict(
 idea='The Creed says the Church is one, holy, catholic and apostolic. These four marks come from Christ and the Spirit, and they help us recognize the Church he founded on Peter and the apostles.',
 hook='Ask, “If you lost your phone, how would you know yours from a lookalike?” Introduce marks as the signs by which we recognize Christ’s Church.',
 activity='“Four marks scavenger hunt” (30 min). Around the parish or in photos, find examples: one (the same Mass in every language), holy (a saint’s statue, the tabernacle), catholic (flags of countries in the parish), apostolic (the bishop’s photo, the list of popes). Record and share.',
 teach=['One: one Lord, one faith, one Baptism (Ephesians 4:4–6; CCC 813–822). Unity does not mean uniformity: many rites and cultures. Wounds to unity exist, and we pray for Christian unity (CCC 817–822).',
        'Holy: because Christ and the Spirit make her holy, though her members are sinners (CCC 823–829). Catholic: whole and for all (CCC 830–856).',
        'Apostolic: founded on the apostles, with their teaching handed on through bishops in succession from them (CCC 857–865). Christ said, “You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church” (Matthew 16:18).',
        'Teach respectfully about other Christians: the Church recognizes much truth and holiness among them (CCC 817–819).'],
 misc=['“If the Church is holy, no Catholic can sin.”', '“Catholic just means a Roman church.”'],
 mem='“You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church.” (Matthew 16:18)',
 assess='Ask children to give each mark and one real sign of it.'),
'4-5-1': dict(
 idea='The communion of saints unites all who belong to Christ: the Church on earth, the souls being purified, and the saints in heaven. We share holy things and pray for one another.',
 hook='Show a family tree with some names in gold. Ask, “Who are the people who are looking out for you?” Add the saints to the list.',
 activity='“Make a litany” (25 min). Each child chooses a saint and writes one invocation (e.g., “St Francis, teach us to love creation”). Combine the lines into a class litany. Pray it together. Read Hebrews 12:1–2.',
 teach=['“Communion of saints” means both holy persons and holy things, above all the Eucharist (CCC 946–948). All members share spiritual goods.',
        'Three states of the Church: pilgrims, souls being purified, the blessed in heaven (CCC 954). The saints intercede for us (CCC 956–957).',
        'We honor saints; we worship God alone. Their lives are models and their prayers help (CCC 2683).',
        'Link to praying for the dead on All Souls (November 2) and visiting cemeteries (CCC 958).'],
 misc=['“Praying to saints is worshiping them.”', '“Saints are far away and don’t care.”'],
 mem='“We are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses.” (Hebrews 12:1)',
 assess='Ask children to explain the difference between honoring saints and worshiping God.'),
'4-5-2': dict(
 idea='“I believe in the forgiveness of sins.” Jesus gave the apostles the power to forgive sins. We receive forgiveness at Baptism, and again in the Sacrament of Reconciliation when we confess, are sorry, and receive absolution.',
 hook='Tell a story of a child who broke a window and hid. How did they feel? What relieved them? “God does not want us to carry sin; he wants to lift it.”',
 activity='“Steps of Confession” (25 min). Arrange cards: examine conscience, be sorry, confess, receive penance, absolution, do the penance. Role-play with a puppet priest or an adult. Practice the Act of Contrition. Read John 20:21–23.',
 teach=['On Easter evening Jesus breathed on the apostles and said, “Whose sins you forgive are forgiven” (John 20:21–23; CCC 976–983). The Church continues this through bishops and priests.',
        'The sacrament has acts of the penitent: contrition, confession, satisfaction, and the priest’s absolution (CCC 1450–1460, 1480). The priest is bound by the seal of confession (CCC 1467).',
        'Mortal sin breaks our friendship with God; venial sin weakens it (CCC 1854–1864). The sacrament restores grace and peace.',
        'Reassure children: Confession is meeting a merciful Father. Prepare them to go without fear.'],
 misc=['“Priests forgive sins on their own power.”', '“I only have to confess if I feel guilty.”'],
 mem='“Whose sins you forgive are forgiven them.” (John 20:23)',
 assess='Ask a child to walk through the steps of Confession in order and explain what each means.'),
'4-5-3': dict(
 idea='“I believe in the resurrection of the body and life everlasting.” Our bodies will rise at the end, transformed like Christ’s. Those who love God will live with him forever in heaven.',
 hook='Show a seed and a flower. Read 1 Corinthians 15:36–44. “What you sow is not the body that shall be.” Ask what might be different about a risen body.',
 activity='“What will heaven be like?” (25 min). Read Revelation 21:1–5 and 1 Corinthians 15. List what Scripture says (no tears, no death, God dwells with us, new heaven and earth). Children sketch it and write a prayer for someone who has died.',
 teach=['At death the soul is separated from the body and judged (CCC 1021–1022). At the end of time the body will rise and be reunited with the soul (CCC 988–1004). Christ’s Resurrection is the pattern (CCC 999).',
        'Heaven is seeing God face to face and sharing his joy (CCC 1023–1029). Purgatory purifies those who die in friendship with God (CCC 1030–1032). Hell is real: free refusal of God (CCC 1033–1037).',
        'The “new heaven and new earth” means creation is renewed, not destroyed (CCC 1042–1050).',
        'Handle the topic of death with warmth. Some children will have lost loved ones.'],
 misc=['“We become angels when we die.”', '“Our bodies are just thrown away at death.”'],
 mem='“Eye has not seen, nor ear heard… what God has prepared for those who love him.” (1 Corinthians 2:9)',
 assess='Ask: “What happens to our bodies at the end? What is heaven?”'),
'4-6-1': dict(
 idea='By a special grace from God, Mary was conceived without original sin. The angel greeted her as “full of grace.” We celebrate the Immaculate Conception on December 8.',
 hook='Show a gift wrapped for a very special person. “If God were to prepare a mother for his Son, what would he do?” Let the children suggest, then connect to Gabriel’s greeting.',
 activity='“Full of grace” (20 min). Read Luke 1:28 in several translations. Discuss why “Hail, full of grace” is almost a name. Then write a card to Mary for December 8. Learn the Hail Mary’s first half.',
 teach=['The dogma (Pius IX, 1854): from the first moment of her conception Mary was preserved from original sin, by the merits of Christ (CCC 490–493). Mary was saved by Christ in the most perfect way, in advance.',
        'Luke 1:28 “kecharitomene,” “full of grace,” points to this fullness (CCC 490–491). Mary is the new Eve whose “yes” undoes Eve’s “no” (CCC 494).',
        'The feast is December 8 and is a holy day of obligation in the U.S. (CCC 1172). Our Lady of Lourdes said, “I am the Immaculate Conception” (1858).',
        'Remember to separate this from Jesus’ virginal conception, a very common confusion.'],
 misc=['“The Immaculate Conception means Jesus’ virgin birth.”', '“Mary did not need a Savior.”'],
 mem='Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord is with thee.',
 assess='Ask: “What does ‘Immaculate Conception’ mean? Whom does it honor, and why?”'),
'4-6-2': dict(
 idea='Mary is the Mother of God because the Son she bore is God. The Council of Ephesus (431) defined this title, “Theotokos,” to protect the truth about Jesus.',
 hook='Ask, “If you are the mother of a king, are you only the mother of the king’s body?” A mother is mother of the whole person. Mary is mother of the whole Jesus, who is God.',
 activity='“Council in session” (30 min). Roleplay the Council of Ephesus: one side says Mary is only mother of Jesus’ humanity, the other that she is Mother of God. Children debate, then the “bishops” read Luke 1:43 (“the mother of my Lord”) and Galatians 4:4 and vote. Draw an icon of the Theotokos.',
 teach=['Mary is the Mother of God, “Theotokos” (CCC 495, 509). The title does not say she is the origin of God but that her Son is God.',
        'Ephesus (431) condemned Nestorius, who separated Jesus into two persons (CCC 466). The title protects the Incarnation.',
        'Elizabeth called her “the mother of my Lord” (Luke 1:43). Paul says God sent his Son “born of woman” (Galatians 4:4).',
        'Mary’s motherhood extends to us (CCC 967–970). Many Eastern icons show her pointing to Jesus.'],
 misc=['“Mary gave Jesus his divinity.”', '“Calling Mary Mother of God makes her equal to God.”'],
 mem='“And why is this granted me, that the mother of my Lord should come to me?” (Luke 1:43)',
 assess='Ask: “Why do we call Mary the Mother of God? What does this tell us about Jesus?”'),
'4-6-3': dict(
 idea='At the end of her earthly life Mary was taken body and soul into heavenly glory (the Assumption, August 15). She shares fully in her Son’s Resurrection and is Queen of Heaven. Her destiny shows what awaits us.',
 hook='Ask, “If you could see a preview of the end of the movie, would you?” Mary’s Assumption is a preview of our own hope.',
 activity='“Marian feasts calendar” (25 min). Create a year strip with Mary’s feasts: Jan 1 (Mother of God), Feb 11 (Lourdes), Mar 25 (Annunciation), May (month of Mary), Aug 15 (Assumption), Aug 22 (Queenship), Sept 8 (Birth), Oct 7 (Rosary), Dec 8 (Immaculate Conception), Dec 12 (Guadalupe). Pray the Salve Regina.',
 teach=['Pius XII defined the Assumption in 1950: Mary, having completed her earthly life, was taken up body and soul into heavenly glory (CCC 966). It is the final fulfillment of her union with Christ.',
        'The sign of the woman clothed with the sun in Revelation 12:1 is read by the Church as Mary and as the Church (CCC 972). The feast has been kept since the early centuries, and the Eastern Church calls it the Dormition.',
        'Mary is Queen because her Son is King (CCC 966, 970). She is a mother who intercedes, not a rival to Christ.',
        'August 15 is a holy day of obligation in the U.S. in most years. Check the calendar.'],
 misc=['“Mary is a goddess.”', '“The Assumption is a late invention with no roots.”'],
 mem='Hail, holy Queen, mother of mercy, our life, our sweetness and our hope.',
 assess='Ask: “What happened to Mary at the end of her life? What does this show about us?”'),
'4-7-1': dict(
 idea='The sacraments are seven effective signs, instituted by Christ and given to the Church, that give us his grace. Each has matter (the sign) and form (the words), and a minister.',
 hook='Set out water, oil, bread, wine, a ring and a stole. Ask, “What do these do?” Hold up the idea that God uses ordinary things to give us his life.',
 activity='“Seven sacraments matrix” (30 min). Build a chart with columns: sacrament, sign (matter), words (form), minister, grace. Children fill rows using cards. Check against the Catechism summaries. Highlight which are received once (Baptism, Confirmation, Orders).',
 teach=['The sacraments are “efficacious signs of grace” (CCC 1131). They work “ex opere operato,” by Christ’s action, not by the minister’s holiness (CCC 1127–1128).',
        'Grouped as initiation (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist), healing (Reconciliation, Anointing), and service of communion (Holy Orders, Matrimony) (CCC 1210–1211).',
        'Three leave a lasting mark (character): Baptism, Confirmation, Holy Orders (CCC 1121).',
        'Receiving them with faith matters (CCC 1128). Grace is a gift, not magic.'],
 misc=['“Sacraments are only symbols.”', '“A priest’s holiness makes the sacrament work.”'],
 mem='The seven sacraments: Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist, Reconciliation, Anointing of the Sick, Holy Orders, Matrimony.',
 assess='Ask children to name the seven, group them, and give the sign for three.'),
'4-7-2': dict(
 idea='Virtues are good habits that help us do what is right. Conscience is the inner judgment that tells us what to do. We grow by practice, prayer and the sacraments.',
 hook='Ask who has learned a skill by practice (piano, free throws). “Doing good is like that. Each good choice makes the next easier.”',
 activity='“Virtue habit tracker” (25 min). Each child picks one virtue (patience, honesty, generosity, courage) and designs a seven-day tracker with a daily action and a Friday reflection. Pair up and promise to check in.',
 teach=['A virtue is “a habitual and firm disposition to do the good” (CCC 1803). Human virtues are acquired by practice; theological virtues (faith, hope, charity) are infused by God (CCC 1804–1813).',
        'The four cardinal virtues: prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance (CCC 1805–1809). Philippians 4:8 lists what to think about.',
        'Conscience is a judgment of reason about the morality of an act. We must always follow it and also form it (CCC 1776–1785).',
        'Vices are bad habits; the seven capital sins lead to others (CCC 1866). Name them without despair: virtue also grows.'],
 misc=['“Virtue is being naturally good.”', '“Conscience can’t be wrong.”'],
 mem='“Whatever is true, whatever is honorable, whatever is just, whatever is pure… think about these things.” (Philippians 4:8)',
 assess='Ask each child to name a virtue, define it and tell how they will practice it.'),
'4-7-3': dict(
 idea='The Ten Commandments and the Beatitudes work together: the commandments show the minimum of love, and the Beatitudes show the heights. Jesus sums up the law: love God and love your neighbor.',
 hook='Show a fence along a mountain road and a view at the top. “The fence keeps us safe; the view is why we go.” The commandments keep us on the road; the Beatitudes show where it leads.',
 activity='“Bridge” build (25 min). Draw a bridge between a commandment and a Beatitude (e.g., “You shall not kill” and “Blessed are the peacemakers”). Each pair makes two or three bridges and explains them. Read Matthew 22:37–40.',
 teach=['The Decalogue was given to Moses after the Exodus, as a gift to a people already rescued (CCC 2056–2063). Jesus came not to abolish but to fulfill the Law (Matthew 5:17; CCC 577–582).',
        'The two tablets: love of God (1–3) and love of neighbor (4–10) (CCC 2067–2068). Jesus says everything hangs on these two commands (Matthew 22:37–40; CCC 2055).',
        'The Beatitudes are the vision of the happiness to which we are called (CCC 1716–1717). Together they teach: “Love and be happy.”',
        'The commandments bind all people, always and everywhere (CCC 2072). They are written on our hearts.'],
 misc=['“The Old Law no longer matters.”', '“Beatitudes are suggestions; the commandments are rules.”'],
 mem='“You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart… and your neighbor as yourself.” (Matthew 22:37–39)',
 assess='Ask: “How do the commandments and Beatitudes fit together? Give one pair.”'),
'4-8-1': dict(
 idea='The Our Father is the prayer Jesus gave us, “the summary of the whole Gospel” (Tertullian). It begins by calling God Father, then asks for seven things: God’s name, kingdom and will first, and then our bread, forgiveness, protection from temptation and deliverance from evil.',
 hook='Say the Our Father extremely fast, then ask what you said. Then slow down: “Jesus gave us each word on purpose.”',
 activity='“Seven petitions” paraphrase (30 min). Write each petition on a card and fold the group around it. Children rewrite it in their own words, draw an action that matches, and share. Read Matthew 6:9–13 and Luke 11:1–4. Pray it together very slowly.',
 teach=['Jesus taught his disciples to pray when they asked (Luke 11:1; Matthew 6:9–13; CCC 2759–2761). Tertullian called it “the summary of the whole Gospel” (CCC 2761).',
        'Calling God “Our Father” (“Abba”) shows adoption in Christ (CCC 2777–2785). “Our” means we pray with and for all (CCC 2786–2793).',
        'The first three petitions are about God (hallowed name, coming kingdom, done will); the last four about us (bread, forgiveness, temptation, evil) (CCC 2803–2854).',
        'Do not rush. The next lessons in later grades examine each petition in depth.'],
 misc=['“God gives bad things to teach me lessons” (from “lead us not into temptation”).', '“Daily bread just means food.”'],
 mem='Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name; thy kingdom come, thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven.',
 assess='Ask each child to say the Our Father and explain one petition in their own words.'),
'4-8-2': dict(
 idea='There are five forms of prayer (blessing and adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise) and three expressions (vocal, meditation, contemplation). The psalms are the Church’s prayer book.',
 hook='Say “Wow,” “Please,” “For you,” “Thank you,” and “Hurray!” Ask which one is prayer. All five are.',
 activity='“Prayer menu” (25 min). Children build a menu of the five forms with a short example of each. Try a Lectio divina with Psalm 63:1–8: read, meditate, pray, rest. Journal a line afterward.',
 teach=['The five forms (CCC 2626–2643): blessing and adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise. Each appears in the psalms.',
        'The three expressions (CCC 2700–2724): vocal prayer uses words, meditation uses thought and imagination, contemplation is silent, loving attention to God.',
        'The Psalms have been the prayer of Jesus and the Church (CCC 2585–2589). We pray them in the Mass and the Liturgy of the Hours.',
        'Teach that dryness and distractions are normal (CCC 2729–2733). Keep praying.'],
 misc=['“Prayer is always asking for things.”', '“If I am distracted, my prayer is worthless.”'],
 mem='“O God, you are my God, I seek you, my soul thirsts for you.” (Psalm 63:1)',
 assess='Ask children to name the five forms and give a short example of each.'),
'4-8-3': dict(
 idea='The Church year unfolds the whole mystery of Christ, and the Mass is its center. The Mass has two main parts: the Liturgy of the Word and the Liturgy of the Eucharist. Jesus meets us in both, as on the road to Emmaus.',
 hook='Read Luke 24:13–35 in short pieces. Ask, “When did the disciples recognize Jesus?” (in the breaking of the bread). Ask which two parts of Mass match “scripture explained” and “bread broken.”',
 activity='“Mass map” (30 min). Fold a long strip into the parts of Mass: Introductory Rites, Liturgy of the Word, Liturgy of the Eucharist, Concluding Rites. Add details for each (Penitential Act, readings, homily, Creed, offertory, Eucharistic Prayer, Communion, blessing). Match to Emmaus. Rehearse the responses.',
 teach=['The liturgical year celebrates the whole mystery of Christ (CCC 1163–1167). Sunday is the center, Easter is the feast of feasts (CCC 1168–1169).',
        'The Mass has four parts: introductory rites, Liturgy of the Word, Liturgy of the Eucharist, concluding rites (CCC 1346–1355). The Emmaus story shows this shape (CCC 1347).',
        'In the Eucharistic Prayer, the bread and wine become Christ’s Body and Blood (CCC 1375–1376). The Mass is the memorial and sacrifice of Calvary made present (CCC 1362–1367).',
        'Encourage active participation: singing, responding and listening (CCC 1141).'],
 misc=['“The Mass is just a reminder of the Last Supper.”', '“The priest does everything; we just watch.”'],
 mem='“Stay with us, for it is toward evening and the day is now far spent.” (Luke 24:29)',
 assess='Ask children to name the four parts of the Mass and one thing that happens in each.'),
}
